{{smaller block|Much other ''trucke'' we had, and after two dayes he came aboord, and did eate and drinke with vs very merrily.
{{right|Quoted in ''Capt. John Smith’s'' Works, I. 82.}}}}

'''3.''' The truck system.
{{smaller block|It is no doubt difficult to work the lumber trade, where gangs of men are despatched great distances, or the fishing trade, without some resort to ''truck''.
{{right|''Sir C. W. Dilke'', Probs. of Greater Britain, i. 2.}}}}

'''4.''' Commodities for barter or trade. (''a'') Small wares; stuff; goods; gear; belongings; hence, rubbish. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|Retaining Tisquantum to send from place to place to procure ''truck'' for us.
{{right|''Mourt’s Journal'', in Appendix to New England’s Memorial, p. 360.}}
They gin’ her a ’bundance of ''truck;'' I don’t know what all; and none of ’em holp her at all.
{{right|''A. B. Longstreet'', Georgia Scenes, p. 192.}}}}
